            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioners, the United States argues that libraries\u2019 blocking of Internet pornography is comparable to the traditional practice of excluding certain books from shelves."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioners",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "a7ca75c23d67a0bc9e3f241e78416f98a178a3730315b36b23fcfacb040bc638",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-a7ca75c23d67-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The petitioners note that the Children\u2019s Internet Protection Act (CIPA) does not prohibit any Internet speech."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Respondents",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "a7ca75c23d67a0bc9e3f241e78416f98a178a3730315b36b23fcfacb040bc638",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-a7ca75c23d67-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 25,
              "start": 25
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Respondents, respondents assert that CIPA is unconstitutional because it requires libraries receiving federal funds to engage in presumptively unconstitutional conduct, invoking strict scrutiny."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Respondents",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "a7ca75c23d67a0bc9e3f241e78416f98a178a3730315b36b23fcfacb040bc638",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-a7ca75c23d67-0001-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 19,
              "start": 19
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Respondents, they argue that challenges to CIPA should be as\u2011applied rather than facial, focusing on specific instances of enforcement."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Smith",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "a7ca75c23d67a0bc9e3f241e78416f98a178a3730315b36b23fcfacb040bc638",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-a7ca75c23d67-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 36,
              "start": 36
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Smith, respondents maintain that the Internet is not analogous to books and should not be treated as a traditional library collection."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Olson",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "a7ca75c23d67a0bc9e3f241e78416f98a178a3730315b36b23fcfacb040bc638",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-a7ca75c23d67-0003-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 56,
              "start": 56
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Olson, data show that about 95\u202f% of libraries with public Internet access already have acceptable\u2011use policies governing patron behavior."
      }
    ],
